{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact 7c9991b58a05cad9a232a821f78a07d830e17f7c and all 24 named artifacts, primary 2ae9967b first, with 70193521 retained as a companion, under review 81c6e809 and promise be11440a. The parent request add29762 and requester dispositions c4d5dce8 and 6cf3efa3 govern this approval. The earlier clock, cancellation, late-answer and interpretation-compatibility findings are resolved for this parent's bounded outcome.\n\nThe browser now retains a projection only when the resident's profile is nonempty and equals the retained Status profile. A different, empty or missing profile drops that projection whether or not a cold audit runs. A matching profile preserves useful availability, qualified during a rebuild. A newly returned verified Status restores availability after a drop. The hash covers exactly the application's projectionProfile identity, including application and fold version. Interpreter selection is immutable per opened Workspace, and the published snapshot records that same identity; there is no second mutable selection that could relabel an old snapshot.\n\nArchitecture: layers 3, 5 and 7 retain their separation. The shared clock owns only head notices, verification and projection remain in the application, and the browser handles qualified display. The additive profile fields on Status and rebuild responses change the wire contract; this exact head updates docs/reference/architecture.md and docs/reference/gs/serve.md, with current candidate provenance to their producers. The six documentation artifacts reach their relevant current implementation artifacts. No architecture change is left undocumented.\n\nSecurity: a retained projection is observational and never authorizes an action. Missing compatibility now fails closed, including the actual unavailable-interpreter producer demonstrated in the prior isolated Workspace probe. The opaque fixed-size profile identifies interpretation compatibility, not actor authority or work data. The shared-clock retirement chain, cancellation ownership, single in-flight probe and late-answer guard preserve the tested bounds. No key, signature, secret or authority boundary change is introduced. This is explicitly assigned work; it does not rely on a participant request masquerading as an adopted decision. No unresolved security defect was found in this scope.\n\nSimplification: one shared clock, one owned probe and one profile-compatibility rule cover the required cases. Removing the previous missing-profile exception simplifies the browser's decision. No additional compatibility state or parallel verifier is needed to satisfy this parent. No required simplification remains.\n\nValidation at this exact head: uncached Go1.26.7 tests pass for internal/app (113.227s), internal/service (62.525s), internal/wireparity (0.793s), cmd/gitseq-mcp (122.863s) and internal/docset (76.925s); all 217 UI tests, targeted vet and reproducible make ui-check pass. All 24 previews are ready and byte-exact. The review checkout is clean. Thirteen independent real-useWorkroom tests driven by controlled endpoint responses pass: known same/different profiles and four unknown-profile combinations, each with running true and false, plus verified recovery. Four actual omissions fail their intended tests: nonempty proof, profile equality, application identity and fold-version identity. Source edits from those experiments were restored. This is isolated producer and hook evidence, not a deployed browser/service upgrade. Full-repository Go validation remains the exact bd6fb383 result for unchanged packages; only the affected packages were rerun here. Prior clock and probe-lifecycle controls and omissions remain evidence for their unchanged code. No new large-depth performance or physical-resource claim is made. The attached independent evidence records these limits and the harmless vet/build overlap in our test orchestration.\n\nThe candidates are live with ordinary inherited staleness